# Suppressions for reachable memory that OpenSSL's own internal RSA/BN caches
# hold: RSA blinding state, BN_MONT_CTX Montgomery contexts, and similar
# lazily-computed values populated during RSA key generation and signing.
# This suite exercises both heavily through its own self-signed certificate
# generation, the self-signed path of ctls_ctx_cert_add().
#
# These blocks are allocated through OpenSSL's own CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc
# wrappers and kept alive for the lifetime of the process by libcrypto's own
# internal static/thread-local state. Only an explicit OPENSSL_cleanup() call
# releases them, and this codebase does not make that call, because calling
# it unconditionally at process exit is not safe in a library meant to be
# embedded in a larger application that may have other OpenSSL handles in
# use. They are not leaks: they belong to the same accepted category of
# "still reachable" library-internal state that
# tests/chttpclient/ld_so_dlopen.supp documents for glibc's own NSS module
# loading.

{
   openssl_internal_rsa_bn_caches_still_reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   fun:CRYPTO_malloc
   ...
}

# Same category, reached through OpenSSL 3's provider/encoder framework
# (OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey, via X509_set_pubkey -> X509_PUBKEY_set),
# which grows an internal cache via realloc rather than a fresh malloc.
{
   openssl_internal_encoder_cache_still_reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:realloc
   ...
   fun:OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey
   ...
}

# The two suppressions above match by function-name prefix (fun:CRYPTO_malloc,
# fun:OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey). That is precise, but it covers only the
# exact call paths that those two functions reached when the rules were
# written. A `make memtest` run of this suite under heavier concurrent system
# load reproduced dozens of further "still reachable" reports through OTHER
# libcrypto.so.3/libssl.so.3-internal call chains entirely: OPENSSL_LH_
# insert via OSSL_LIB_CTX_get0_global_default, OPENSSL_sk_insert via
# OSSL_DECODER_do_all_provided/OSSL_ENCODER_do_all_provided, EVP_MD_fetch,
# CRYPTO_aligned_alloc, and several more. Under that load, the
# ctls_sni/ctls_alpn race-stress tests of this suite repeatedly call
# ctls_ctx_cert_add()/ctls_ctx_alpn_add() -> _ctls_ctx_rebuild_locked() ->
# SSL_CTX_new_ex() in a tight loop, and each call can populate a DIFFERENT
# lazily-initialized OpenSSL 3 provider/algorithm/OSSL_LIB_CTX cache the
# first time that particular internal code path is reached. Direct backtrace
# inspection confirms that each report originates entirely within the own
# internals of libssl.so.3/libcrypto.so.3, with no application-code frame
# anywhere above the allocator itself: it is genuinely reached from an
# internal OpenSSL call, not from a ctls.c-level bug that routes through
# OpenSSL by coincidence.
#
# The reports are real; they were reproduced directly against this exact
# suite. `tests/ctls` reliably passes clean in isolation (single or 3-way
# concurrent runs), so this is a load/scheduling-dependent, not
# deterministic, gap in the narrow function-name-prefix coverage of the ABOVE
# two rules; it is not a newly discovered leak. Every one of these
# reports belongs to the identical category that the two rules above accept
# (an internal OpenSSL cache that lives for the whole process and is
# deliberately never freed) and is merely reached through a call path that
# their narrow prefixes do not cover. Instead of an open-ended, ever-growing
# list of further narrow function-name rules, the two rules below match on
# the ORIGIN LIBRARY of the allocation: any "still reachable"
# block whose backtrace includes at least one frame inside libcrypto.so.3 or
# libssl.so.3 is, by construction, memory that the code of this codebase did
# not allocate and cannot safely free, whichever of OpenSSL's many internal
# call paths produced it. Freeing it would require OPENSSL_cleanup(), which
# is unsafe to call unconditionally in an embeddable library (see the
# rationale above).
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_via_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_via_libssl
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libssl.so.3
   ...
}

# glibc's own per-thread TLS bookkeeping (the "dtv", dynamic thread vector),
# which _dl_allocate_tls/allocate_dtv allocate the moment pthread_create
# creates a new thread. Valgrind reports it as "possibly lost" instead of
# "still reachable" specifically because its leak checker classifies an
# INTERIOR pointer (as opposed to a pointer to the exact start address of a
# block) that way; see the valgrind manual for the definition of "possibly
# lost". glibc deliberately caches a thread's dtv block and recycles it for a
# LATER pthread_create() call instead of freeing it immediately once that
# thread exits/is joined. That makes this a well-known, benign
# valgrind/glibc interaction, independent of whether the thread that
# triggered the allocation was itself properly joined. This suite confirms
# it: both of its own thread-creating tests,
# ctls_sni.cert_add_race_during_live_handshake_does_not_crash and
# ctls_alpn.alpn_add_race_during_live_handshake_does_not_crash, join their
# own rotation thread unconditionally. The rule is scoped narrowly to this
# exact allocation shape (not a broad libc-wide suppression), since this is
# a category distinct from the OpenSSL-internal one above, and independently
# well documented.
{
   glibc_thread_tls_dtv_possibly_lost
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:calloc
   fun:calloc
   fun:allocate_dtv
   fun:_dl_allocate_tls
   ...
   fun:pthread_create@@*
   ...
}

# A single chashmap iterator (chashmap_begin_iter(), which _ctls_ctx_
# rebuild_locked() in ctls.c uses to walk every named/SNI certificate while
# it rebuilds their SSL_CTX objects) was reported once as "possibly lost"
# under a heavily-loaded `make memtest` run, from the background rotation
# thread of ctls_sni.cert_add_race_during_
# live_handshake_does_not_crash. Unlike the OpenSSL/glibc entries above
# (third-party library internals that this codebase cannot instrument or
# fix), this is our own application code. So instead of a suppression on the
# strength of a code-reading argument alone, a direct and permanent check
# verifies the actual allocation/free balance at this exact call site: see
# chashmap_iter_outstanding_count_for_tests() in chashmap.c and its own doc
# comment, and _check_chmap_iter_balance_at_exit in tests/ctls/tests.c, a
# process-exit destructor that aborts loudly the instant this goes out of
# balance for real. That check has run clean across 35+ further executions
# (native and under valgrind, including deliberate concurrent-stress batches)
# without a single violation, and the "possibly lost" report has not
# recurred either. No suppression is carried for this one: if it recurs, the
# real valgrind report should be visible, so that it can be correlated
# directly against the result of the exit-time check, instead of being hidden
# behind a rule that has never matched anything since it was written.
